From spencer.graves at PDF.COM  Fri Aug  1 14:00:33 2003
From: spencer.graves at PDF.COM (Spencer Graves)
Date: Fri, 01 Aug 2003 07:00:33 -0700
Subject: Re: logistic regression
References: <DDE6D85109A0D611B5EB000103E234BD1F66A6@s1ffcd4.ba.ssa.gov>
Message-ID: <3F2A7281.8030601@pdf.com>

	  To test ideas, it is still quite reasonable to transform the numbers as,

	  logits <- log(y/(1-y))

where 0 < y < 1.  One can make normal probability plots of "logits", 
plot them vs. potentially explanatory variables, use them as the 
response in "lm", etc.  If there are a few 0's and 1's (but not very 
many), then I'm not above shrinking y towards 0.5 by first computing

	  y.shrink = c0*(y-0.5)

where c0 is a number close to 1;  if the y's are proportions with 
denominator n, then I might let c0 = 1-0.5/n.

	  If many of the y's are 0's or 1's or if I need an answer for a 
publication, then I will do my final model fits using "glm" with family 
= "binomial", as described in ch. 7 of a book with a bright yellow and 
blue cover by Venables and Ripley (2002) Modern Applied Statistics with 
S, 4th ed. (Springer);  I've found this book to be quite valuable in 
helping me get things done wiht S.

hope this helps.  spencer graves

