From Karen_Byron at bd.com  Mon Aug  4 18:55:12 2003
From: Karen_Byron at bd.com
Date: Mon, 04 Aug 2003 14:55:12 -0400
Subject: Stirling Numbers
Message-ID: <OF4ED46862.2A1A142E-ON85256D75.00716FEC@bd.com>

I am running Professional Edition Version 6.0.2 Release 1 for Microsoft
Windows : 2001 under Windows 2000 Professional.

I am implementing a non-parmetric homoscedasticity test.
The test was described in Goldfeld and Quandt's 1965 JASA article 60
():539:547
As part of the test I needed the unsigned Stirling numbers of the first
kind
     simple definition: the absolute value of the coefficients generated by
the expansion  (x)sub n = x (x - 1) (x - 2) ... (x - n + 1)
     see the link for a more complete definition:
http://mathworld.wolfram.com/StirlingNumberoftheFirstKind.html

I wrote 2 functions that generate Stirling numbers for a given n.
The first one is called stirling.recursive.  It is similar to what a person
would do with a pencil and piece of paper.  I never got the memory
limitations I expected, but after n = 13 some of the numbers are NA's.
The second function is called stirling and uses matrix multiplication.  On
my system it could only multiply ~70 matrixes at once so I set a limit of
50.  The function is good to n = 170, after that many of the numbers
returned are Inf.

The functions are below.  I am interested in
      1)  Any suggestions that improve the function stirling
      2)  Insight as to why stirling.recursive returns NA's

I have verified that the function stirling produces the correct numbers for
the published values I have.    If it could be useful to someone else be my
guest.
s(n, k) = stirling(n)[k]

I will summarize responses to the list.
KJB

stirling.recursive <- function(n, signed = F){
# n is a postive integer
# uses recursion
   if(n == 1){
     stir <- 1
   }
   else{
     stir <- c(1, -1)
   }
   if(n > 2){
     stir.new <- stir
     for(i in 2:(n - 1)){
        stir <- stir.new
        stir.new <- stir %o% c(1, -1 * i)
        nRow <- dim(stir.new)[1]
        stir.new <- c(stir.new[1, 1], stir.new[-1, 1] + stir.new[-nRow, 2],
          stir.new[nRow, 2])
     }
     stir <- stir.new
   }
   if(!signed){
     stir <- abs(stir)
   }
   return(rev(stir))
}

stirling <- function(n, signed = F){
# n is a postive integer
# works because
# x *  (x - 1) * (x - 2) *     (x - 3) *         (x - 4)   can be calc as
   matrix product
# |1|  |1  -1|   |1  -2   0|   |1  -3   0   0|   |1  -4   0   0   0|
#                |0   1  -2|   |0   1  -3   0|   |0   1  -4   0   0|
#                              |0   0   1  -3|   |0   0   1  -4   0|
#                                                |0   0   0   1  -4|

   if(n < 1 | round(n, 0) != n) {
     stop("n must be a postive integer")
   }

   stir <- 1
   if(n > 1){
     limit <- 50
     a.list <- lapply(1:(n - 1), function(x)
        cbind(diag(x), rep(0, x)) + cbind(rep(0, x), -1 * x * diag(x)))
     nSet <- ceiling((n - 1) / limit)
     for(i in 1:nSet){
        list.low <- (i - 1) * limit + 1
        list.high <- min(n - 1, i * limit)
        a.text <- paste(paste(
          "a.list[[", list.low:list.high, "]]", sep = ""), collapse = " %*%
   ")
        stir.inter <- eval(parse(text = a.text))
        stir <- stir %*% stir.inter
        NULL
     }
   }

   if(!signed) {
     stir <- abs(stir)
   }
   return(rev(stir))
}

