Message-ID: <000a01c35b52$3f08e7e0$ae378651@vsn.local>

Hi all,

 

Following our recent course announcements, I received
a number of questions regarding the structure of our S-PLUS training courses,
especially our forthcoming “S-PLUS for Pharma” and “S-PLUS
for Finance” courses.  Although
we run a number of public courses in the UK, we have delivered
on-site S/R courses throughout the world; if there is sufficient interest, we would
run courses in other locations.

 

A list of the S-PLUS training courses we could deliver
can be found at http://www.mango-solutions.com/private%20courses.htm.  If you are interested in onsite training,
the courses listed should be seen as “templates” (we typically
design the course using your datasets, ensuring that we cover the topics you
need to cover).  Let me know if you
have any questions regarding any of our S/R training courses ….

 

 

As I mentioned, we have had a great deal of
interest in our “S-PLUS for Pharma” and “S-PLUS for Finance”
courses.  To give you an idea of the
topics covered in these “focused” courses, I wanted to expand on one
of the courses’ contents.  I’ve
picked “S-PLUS for Pharma” in this instance; the “S-PLUS for
Finance” course has a similar “sector” focus (let me know if
you want more information on the “S-PLUS for Finance” course).

 

The “S-PLUS for Pharma” course has been
developed in discussion with a number of key S-PLUS users in the pharmaceutical
industry.  These users have also
supplied pharmaceutical datasets on which the course can be based.  The aim of the course is to introduce
the S language using pharmaceutical examples.  I have listed the course contents below,
and explained a little about each section.

 

Introduction to
the S language and the S community - This section
will introduce S-PLUS and the S language, and speak about how it is typically
used in the pharmaceutical industry. 
I will draw comparisons with the SAS software system, and talk about its
use in early phase (to illustrate this, I will perform some basic graphical
demos using the S-PLUS interface). 
The introduction to the S community will highlight libraries from key
users such as Brian Ripley, Bill Venables, Frank Harrell,
Jose Pinheiro, Scott Emerson, Joe
Schafer and Terry Therneau,
as well as introducing the various online resources that are available (S-News
etc).

 

Working with S – This section will introduce the basic S syntax and will
discuss the way we work in the S-PLUS environment (introducing key topics such
as “search paths” and “frames”).  We will look at topics such as “masking”,
“key strokes in the command window” etc so people get a “feel”
for programming in S.

 

S data objects -
This is a basic but vital section of the
course, which introduces 5 base data structures: vectors, matrices, arrays,
data frames and lists.  The section
goes on to investigate the relationships between the data objects, and will
also use pharmaceutical examples in order to extract and manipulate data (eg. get me all patients whose blood pressure was more than
“X”).  Parallels with
SAS will also be drawn, and “replacing the data step” will be
discussed in terms of merging, subsetting etc.  The section will finish with
introductions to the data import/export functions, and we will import
pharmaceutical datasets in SAS/Excel format for use in the rest of the course.

 

Using S
functions - This section will concentrate on
the basic structure of an S function. 
We will see how to get help on a function, use a function, see a
function, edit a function etc.  This
section will also contain a session on “25 useful S functions”,
which will introduce 25 functions a user may or may not be familiar with.  This list of functions is a very useful
section that is often used in our more advanced courses, and has evolved
through our experience with the S-PLUS software.  Note: This section will use
“statistical” functions, but not “model fitting”
functions as these are covered later in the course.

 

The
"apply" family of functions - Key
to using S are the “apply” family of functions which (in our
opinion) and not covered well by the S-PLUS help system.  This section will introduce control
structures such as loops, and investigate performance issues.  From this we will discuss the
“need” for the apply functions.  This will be followed by a session on
the use of the 5 “apply” functions (apply, lapply,
sapply, tapply and by),
drawing parallels to SAS functionality such as “by” statements and
loops.  Of equal importance here, we
will cover the handling of outputs from the apply family of functions, and also
troubleshoot this functionality (as problems often arise in terms of scoping
rules etc).

 

Simulations – This section will build on the last section, and get users
building simulations from pharmaceutical examples.  This section will be more of a
“workshop” session, where problems will be set, and results
compared.  From this section, users
will gain an understanding of how to “think” in a matrix-based
fashion, as well as an understanding of how apply functions could be used to
optimize S code.

 

Writing S
functions – This section will introduce the
building blocks of S function writing, and briefly compare their functionality
with SAS macros.  We will look at
the basic tools such as required/optional/dummy arguments, using the “match.call” function, error handing and returning
results.  From this standpoint we
will start to discuss the object oriented nature of S, and start to construct a
generic method for a “pharma” class.  This suite of functionality will be
built upon throughout the rest of the course (eg. we
will write a “plot” function for our class in a later section).

 

Pharma
Statistics – Firstly, this section
will look at fitting a basic linear regression to some pharmaceutical
data.  This will be used to
introduce the basic concepts of the S model fitting.  We will fit a model, evaluate a model,
summarize a model, get diagnostic plots from a model, update a model and
extract needed information from the model. 
>From this basis, we will introduce statistical functionality that is
useful in the pharmaceutical world, as well as examples that are typically poor
in SAS.  Topics include: linear
models, mixed models, survival analysis, missing data analysis and general
additive models.  We will then
discuss other statistical topics as directed by course attendees.

 

Creating
pharmaceutical Graphics – In this
section, we will introduce the basic concepts of producing graphical output in
S (including graphical devices, high level functions, low level functions, and
exporting graphics).  We will build
on this to introduce “typical” pharmaceutical graphics such as line
plots and forest plots.  The class
will cover the production of graphical reports by showing how tables and text
can be added to graphical devices. 
This section will then change to a “workshop” session, where
attendees will get the chance to recreate specific examples and write a generic
“plot” function for our “pharma”
class.  We will then go on to
introduce S-PLUS Graphlets for interactive graphical
output.

 

Using S-PLUS – The final section will discuss the many ways in which S
functionality could be deployed. 
The section will concentrate on writing “libraries” and
“modules”, and look at existing examples by introducing the “xPose” library and “seqTrial”
module.  As well as the creation of
new modules/libraries, this section will discuss the ways in which S
functionality could be embedded into other environments such as Excel and web
pages to build pharmaceutical applications.  Other topics include “running S in
batch”, “using runtime licenses”, “server
applications”, “An Introduction to R” and “using S-PLUS
with SAS”.

 

 

Let me know if you have any questions …

 

