From rich at mango-solutions.com  Tue Aug  5 22:36:01 2003
From: rich at mango-solutions.com
Date: Tue, 05 Aug 2003 23:36:01 +0100
Subject: Re: using sapply
In-Reply-To: <C497480353EF904FBF7A8B0851E5422C9F7B7F@MAIL002.AD.MLP.COM>
References: <C497480353EF904FBF7A8B0851E5422C9F7B7F@MAIL002.AD.MLP.COM>
Message-ID: <000001c35ba1$f4354560$28a4fc3e@vsn.local>

Here are 3 versions … not sure how large
your vectors are in real life, so I can’t comment on how they scale …

Version 1 is a loop, version 2 uses eval/parse, and version 3 uses the apply functions … 

 

# Set
up my data 

inIndex
<- c(1,11,17); endIndex <- c(5,14,19)

returns1 <- returns2 <-
returns3 <-
c(1,1.15,1.3,1.5,0.99,0.97,1,1,1.2,1.3,1.4,1.5,0.99,1.2,1.3,1.4,1.5,1.6,0.99,0.92)

 

# Version 1 (Loop)

for
(i in 1:length(inIndex))
returns1[inIndex[i]:endIndex[i]] <- cumprod(returns1[inIndex[i]:endIndex[i]])

 

# Version 2 (Pasting)

mySeqs
<- paste(inIndex, endIndex,
sep=":")

myComs
<- paste("returns2[", mySeqs, "]
<- cumprod(returns2[", mySeqs,
"])")

eval(parse(text=myComs))

 

# Version 3 (Apply)

indList
<- sapply(1:length(inIndex),
function(i, iD, eD) iD[i]:eD[i], iD=inIndex, eD=endIndex)

myList
<- sapply(indList,
function(mySeq, data) data[mySeq]
<- cumprod(data[mySeq]),
data="">

returns3[unlist(indList)] <- unlist(myList)

 

Hope this helps,

