Date: Wed, 06 Aug 2003 10:48:37 -0400
Subject: Re: Logistic Regression Using Survey Weights: Testing for Significance of Variables
In-Reply-To: <OFFAFC3F67.670E219C-ONCC256D7A.0010BF86@moh.govt.nz>
References: <OFFAFC3F67.670E219C-ONCC256D7A.0010BF86@moh.govt.nz>
Message-ID: <000701c35c29$d20c6cb0$1f01a8c0@creahppav>

Hi,

I have not worked in this field for a while, but as far as I remember, it
was common practice when testing for significance in logistic regression or
even chi-square tests and using survey weights, to adjust the weights so
that their sum equals the sample size instead of the population size (I
don't recall the exact terminology for this procedure). This keeps the
relative importance of each observation with regards to the survey design
while avoiding the problem of sample size sensitivity of statistical tests.
Hope this helps.

Michel

