(x)sub n = x (x - 1) (x - 2) ... (x - n + 1)

Two candidate functions were posted.
One, stirling.recursive, returned NA's once n was larger than 13.  Chuck
Taylor pointed out this was due to exceeding the integer limit.  This can
be overcome by using 1. instead of 1 and the as.numeric() function.
   > .Machine$integer.max
   [1] 2147483647
   > .Machine$integer.max + 1
   [1] NA
   > as.numeric(.Machine$integer.max) + 1
   [1] 2147483648
   > .Machine$integer.max + 1.
   [1] 2147483648

The second, stirling, used a list of matrixes that were combined in groups
   of 50.
Bill Venables suggested an alternative that improved the clarity and the
   performance.
stirling.bv <- function(n) {
        v <- c(1., 0)
        if(n <= 1) return(v)
        for(j in 1:(n - 1))
                v <- c(v, 0) - j * c(0, v)
        rev(abs(v))[-1]
}

Looking over a range of n stirling.bv is consistently quicker than the
   other two.
x <- seq(10, 170, by = 10)
a <- array(dim = c(length(x), 3), dimnames = list(x, c('recursive',
   'mat.prod', 'bv')))
for(i in seq(along = x)){
   a[i, 1] <- dos.time(stirling.recursive(x[i]))
   a[i, 2] <- dos.time(stirling(x[i]))
   a[i, 3] <- dos.time(stirling.bv(x[i]))
}
a
