From Rory.Martin at mpi.com  Mon Aug 11 17:23:18 2003
From: Rory.Martin at mpi.com (Martin, Rory)
Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2003 13:23:18 -0400
Subject: CART and undefined values of predictors
Message-ID: <48696A0E44C5684787D9B2BF0EA2C3EE0A12D0@US-VS1.corp.mpi.com>

For certain types of data, predictor variables may be defined only for
subsets of the observations.  For example, for clinical studies
hypertension is a known risk factor for an outcome of myocardial
infarction, but age-at-onset for hypertension (the age at which the
subject developed the condition) is defined only for those subjects who
have developed hypertension.

How should I handle this situation using the CART algorithm?  Does it
make sense to treat observations with undefined values of a predictor in
the same way as "missing" cases?  i.e. use surrogate variables for node
assignment.  Or should I be treating them as censored somehow?

For categorical predictors one can create an additional "undefined"
category, but the situation seems more complex for predictors that are
continuous.  I could transform such predictors from continuous to
categorical, but this doesn't seem a good solution.  I was wondering if
I could impose a cost on such a predictor such that it would only be
used to split a node when the majority of observations in the node had a
defined value for the predictor?

Any thoughts or suggestions gratefully received.

Thanks
Rory

Rory Martin, PhD
Sr Scientist
Millennium Pharmaceuticals

