From joseph468 at yahoo.com  Tue Aug 12 12:55:09 2003
From: joseph468 at yahoo.com (joseph)
Date: Tue, 12 Aug 2003 05:55:09 -0700
Subject: Summary: Vectorizing nested for loops.
Message-ID: <20030812125509.84225.qmail@web9204.mail.yahoo.com>

I wish to acknowledge and thank all those who
responded to my problem of vectorizing nested ?for?
loops (see summary below).  In particular, I wish to
thank Jeffrey Wang, Ted Wright, Tony Plate, John Fox,
Jim Pratt, Mark Leeds, Patrick Burns and James
Holtman.  The ?consensus? solution appears to be the
outer function.  James Holtman, also showed that it
was very fast when vector size was large. Patrick
Burns raises an issue about memory, but I do not know
of any easy way of testing this. Outer works well, and
I thank all those who responded for their time and
effort.

JoeWest

Dear list members:
The following code is a reduced version of a larger
problem:
  
x <- c(5,6,7,8)

y <- c(1,2,3,4)

z <- matrix(NA, nrow=length(x), ncol=length(y))

for(i in 1:4){
        
        for(j in 1:4){
                
                z[i,j] <- abs(x[i]-y[j])
        }
}

z

# Result:
> z
     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] 
[1,]    4    3    2    1
[2,]    5    4    3    2
[3,]    6    5    4    3
[4,]    7    6    5    4

In the larger version, length of both x and y may
approach 1000 (Note that x and y will always be equal
in length, and in some cases x and y may be
identical).

My problem is with the nested for-loop structure.  As
the vector lengths become larger, the code takes much 
more time to run. Many of the S+ references advise
vectorizing the code to avoid the inefficent nested
loops.  I?ve attempted to use the ?apply? family of
functions (lapply, sapply, tapply, rapply?) but can?t
seem to get anything to work.  In The Basics of S+
3ed., Krause and Olson mention that there may be some
situations where this code structure cannot be
avoided. Is this such a case, and if so should I try
to use For instead of for to improve time and memory
use? I am running S+ 6.0 Release 2 on a W2K machine
with 512 MB RAM 800MHz PIII.

Thanks for any help or insights.

Joe West
joseph468 at yahoo.com

James Holtman, Tony Plate, and John Fox all suggested
the outer function:

> x <- c(5,6,7,8)
>
> y <- c(1,2,3,4)
> z <- outer(x, y, function(x,y)abs(x-y))
> z
     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4]
[1,]    4    3    2    1
[2,]    5    4    3    2
[3,]    6    5    4    3
[4,]    7    6    5    4

James Holtman showed how to measure the speed:

For vectors of 1000, it takes less than 2 seconds.
> x<- 1:1000
> y <- 1:1000
> system.time(z <- outer(x, y, function(x,y)abs(x-y)))
[1] 1.80 0.07 1.91   NA   NA

Patrick Burns raised the memory issue:

