Subject: Asymptote in graphs
Message-ID: <003901c36469$544975f0$2032b583@qut.edu.au>

Dear All,

I am wondering how people deal with asymptote in graphs elegantly in Splus?

For example if I am plotting a y=tan(x) graph for x=0 to 2*pi (radians) I
may use:

plot(seq(0,2*pi,length=10000),tan(seq(0,2*pi,length=10000)),type="l")

Obviously this is not quite the right graph as one encounters asymptotes at
two places in the graph.

I suppose it is possible to break this into three sections and put the lines
in separately:

plot(seq(0,2*pi,length=10000),tan(seq(0,2*pi,length=10000)),type="n")
delta<-10e-10

lines(seq(0,pi/2-delta,length=10000),tan(seq(0,pi/2-delta,length=10000)))
lines(seq(pi/2+delta,3*pi/2-delta,length=10000),tan(seq(pi/2+delta,3*pi/2-de
lta,length=10000)))
lines(seq(3*pi/2+delta,2*pi-delta,length=10000),tan(seq(3*pi/2+delta,2*pi-de
lta,length=10000)))

However, is there a much better way to account for this type of problems?

